#!/usr/bin/env perl

# @author Masahiro Kasahara (masahiro@kasahara.ws)

use strict;
use Term::ANSIColor;

while(<>) {
	chomp;
	if(/remark \#\d+:/) {
		print color('blue') . $_ . color('reset') . "\n";
	} elsif(/: warning(\s+\#\d+)?:/) {
		print color('green') . $_ . color('reset') . "\n";
	} elsif(/In function/) {
		print color('yellow') . $_ . color('reset') . "\n";
	} elsif(/undefined reference/) {
		s|undefined reference to \`(.*?)'|color('red') . 'undefined reference to ' . $1 . color('reset')|e;
		print $_ . "\n";
	} elsif(/^(icpc|icc)\s+/) {
		print color('cyan') . $_ . color('reset') . "\n";
	} elsif(/:( catastrophic)? error(\s+\#\d+)?:/) {
		print color('red') . $_ . color('reset') . "\n";
	} elsif(/^$/) {
		# skip
	} else {
		print $_ . "\n";
	}
}

__END__

=pod

=head1 NAME

icc-color - colorize the output of the Intel C/C++ compiler (icc/icpc)

=head1 SYNOPSIS

icc-color

    icc  -O2 hello.c -o hello 2>&1 | icc-color
    icpc -O2 hello.cc -o hello 2>&1 | icc-color

=head1 DESCRIPTION

B<icc-color> reads the diagnostic messages of the Intel C/C++ compiler
(B<icc> and B<icpc>) from standard input, adds ANSI color escape sequences
to make the messages easier to scan, and writes the result to standard output.

Because the compiler prints its diagnostics to standard error, you normally
need to redirect standard error to standard output (B<2E<gt>&1>) before piping
into B<icc-color>.

The following categories of lines are highlighted:

=over 8

=item B<Remarks>

Lines matching C<remark #NNN:> are shown in blue.

=item B<Warnings>

Lines matching C<: warning:> (optionally with a C<#NNN> number) are shown in green.

=item B<Function context>

Lines matching C<In function> are shown in yellow.

=item B<Undefined references>

For linker C<undefined reference to `symbol'> lines, the message and the
referenced symbol are shown in red.

=item B<Compiler command lines>

Lines beginning with B<icc> or B<icpc> are shown in cyan.

=item B<Errors>

Lines matching C<: error:> or C<: catastrophic error:> (optionally with a
C<#NNN> number) are shown in red.

=back

Empty lines are skipped, and all other lines are passed through unchanged.

=head1 SEE ALSO

B<gcc-color> - the equivalent tool for the GNU Compiler Collection.

=head1 AUTHOR

Masahiro Kasahara (masahiro@kasahara.ws)

=cut

